  "fw.posture.applied.accept": "Posture changed to allowing by default.",
  "fw.posture.survival.title": "What always keeps getting through",
  "fw.posture.survival.subtitle": "With the posture set to block, these lines go above everything else — otherwise “block everything” would mean blocking you too. They come from this machine, not from an example list.",
  "fw.posture.survival.error": "I could not build this chain's list: {erro}",
  "fw.posture.survival.established.what": "Connections that were already open",
  "fw.posture.survival.established.why": "Without this line, blocking does not block “what you did not allow”: it drops everything, including every download and call in progress.",
  "fw.posture.survival.related.what": "Error and control replies for connections",
  "fw.posture.survival.related.why": "This is what keeps “packet too big” getting back to you. Without it, connections hang halfway with no message at all.",
  "fw.posture.survival.dnat.what": "Your port forwards",
  "fw.posture.survival.dnat.why": "Without this line, every redirect is still translated and dies right after — the symptom is “the forward stopped working”, with nothing pointing at the firewall.",
  "fw.posture.survival.loopback.what": "The machine talking to itself",
  "fw.posture.survival.loopback.why": "The panel listening on 127.0.0.1 would be unreachable even through an SSH tunnel.",
  "fw.posture.survival.icmpv6.what": "IPv6 neighbour discovery",
  "fw.posture.survival.icmpv6.why": "Without it IPv6 dies entirely, and only IPv6 — IPv4 keeps working, which is exactly why nobody connects the symptom to the firewall.",
  "fw.posture.survival.admin.what": "Your administrative access",
  "fw.posture.survival.admin.why": "SSH and this panel, on the ports this machine actually uses.",
  "fw.posture.survival.dhcpServed.what": "The DHCP you serve to your network",
  "fw.posture.survival.dhcpServed.why": "Without it devices stop getting an IP — and everyone on the network will say “the internet is down”.",
  "fw.posture.survival.dnsServed.what": "The DNS you serve to your network",
  "fw.posture.survival.dnsServed.why": "Without it nothing resolves names, even with the network working perfectly.",
  "fw.posture.survival.dhcpClient.what": "The firewall getting its own address",
  "fw.posture.survival.dhcpClient.why": "The WAN's DHCP renewal does not go through conntrack. The symptom shows up days after a link flap, as “the internet went down by itself on Wednesday”.",
  "fw.posture.confirm.drop": "Block {alvo} by default? From then on only what you have allowed gets through. You have 90 seconds to confirm you can still get in; if you do not confirm, LinkGuard undoes it by itself.",
