  "recipes.start.heading": "Getting started",
  "recipes.start.heading.done": "All set 🎉",
  "recipes.start.subtitle": "Let's turn this machine into a firewall — each step is explained as you go.",
  "recipes.start.subtitle.done": "Your firewall is configured. You can hide this guide.",
  "recipes.start.hide": "Hide guide",
  "recipes.start.progress": "{feitos} of {total}",
  "recipes.start.iface.title": "Identify your connections",
  "recipes.start.iface.body": "Every firewall machine has at least two network connections.",
  "recipes.start.iface.cta": "View interfaces",
  "recipes.start.iface.help.title": "WAN and LAN",
  "recipes.start.iface.help.body": "The *WAN* is your connection to the *internet* (the cable from the provider or modem). The *LAN* is your *local network* (the devices at home). The firewall sits between the two, protecting the LAN and sharing the internet.",
  "recipes.start.wan.title": "Set up your internet (WAN)",
  "recipes.start.wan.body": "Say which interface carries your internet. There can be more than one (multi-WAN).",
  "recipes.start.wan.cta": "Configure WAN",
  "recipes.start.wan.help.title": "WAN link",
  "recipes.start.wan.help.body": "It is the port the internet comes in through. With *two WANs*, LinkGuard can balance usage and switch over automatically if one goes down (*failover*).",
  "recipes.start.nat.title": "Turn it into a firewall (NAT)",
  "recipes.start.nat.body": "Shares the internet with your local network, safely.",
  "recipes.start.nat.cta": "View firewall",
  "recipes.start.nat.help.title": "NAT / routing",
  "recipes.start.nat.help.body": "*NAT* lets many devices in your home share a single internet connection, hidden behind the firewall. It is what turns this machine into a *router*.",
  "recipes.start.dhcp.title": "Hand out IPs (DHCP)",
  "recipes.start.dhcp.body": "Gives an automatic address to every device that joins your network.",
  "recipes.start.dhcp.cta": "Configure DHCP",
  "recipes.start.dhcp.help.title": "DHCP",
  "recipes.start.dhcp.help.body": "Without DHCP you would have to set the IP of every phone, TV and PC by hand. *DHCP* does it on its own. You can still *reserve* a fixed IP for one specific device.",
  "recipes.start.dns.title": "Resolve names (DNS)",
  "recipes.start.dns.body": "Translates \"google.com\" into the real address — and can filter sites.",
  "recipes.start.dns.cta": "Configure DNS",
  "recipes.start.dns.help.title": "DNS",
  "recipes.start.dns.help.body": "*DNS* is the \"phone book\" of the internet: it turns names (_google.com_) into numbers (IPs). Through LinkGuard you see what each device reaches and can *block* domains.",
  "recipes.start.sec.title": "Protect access to the panel",
  "recipes.start.sec.body": "Create your own user and change the default password before running this for real.",
  "recipes.start.sec.cta": "Create my user",
  "recipes.start.sec.help.title": "Panel security",
  "recipes.start.sec.help.body": "The default account is *admin/admin* — anyone on the network could log in. Create *a user of your own* and change the password. You can give each person different permissions.",
